# Dreamscale

Cloud robot policy inference — one function call.

> Pre-alpha SDK. Model access and deployment readiness are separate from package installation.
>
> Formerly `dropbear`. Existing `dropbear` projects keep working unchanged: the
> `dropbear` package now installs `dreamscale` and keeps the old import and command.

## Install

Add Dreamscale to a Python 3.11–3.14 project:

```bash
uv add "dreamscale==0.1.0a272"
```

Install the CLI as a standalone tool:

```bash
uv tool install dreamscale
dreamscale --version
dreamscale login
```

`dreamscale --version` says when a newer release is out; update with
`uv tool upgrade dreamscale`.

The base package contains cloud inference, transport, model contracts, and
robot-neutral observation helpers. Commands install the optional stack they need the
first time they run, using the same installer as Dreamscale itself (`uv tool`, `uv
add`, `uv pip` or `pip`), then continue: SO-101 setup and commands add `so101`; YAM
setup and commands add `yam` and `cameras` (plus `realsense` where it has wheels:
x86_64 Linux and Windows, and aarch64 Linux such as Jetson, where it installs
Dreamscale's RSUSB build of pyrealsense2); `dreamscale sim libero` adds `sim`;
`--trace-dir` adds `trace`; and
`dreamscale integrations hud setup` adds `hud`; `dreamscale integrations inspect-robots setup`
adds `inspect-robots`. A uv tool install moves to Python 3.12
or 3.13 when a stack needs it.

To add a stack to your own project for use from Python:

```bash
uv add "dreamscale[so101]==0.1.0a272"
uv add "dreamscale[sim]==0.1.0a272"
uv add "dreamscale[dreamzero]==0.1.0a272"
uv add "dreamscale[yam,cameras,realsense]==0.1.0a272"
uv add "dreamscale[hud]==0.1.0a272"
```

The `dreamzero` extra installs the pinned H.264 codec used automatically by
DreamZero-YAM's three persistent camera streams. DreamZero-DROID and all other
models keep their existing JPEG transport and do not require this extra.

The base SDK supports Python 3.11 through 3.14. The `so101` extra currently
supports Python 3.12 and 3.13 because its pinned LeRobot dependency requires
Python 3.12 and does not yet provide a wheel-compatible Python 3.14 dependency
stack.

Local LIBERO simulation is qualified on Linux/WSL2 and on Apple Silicon macOS
with Python 3.12. Inference runs in the cloud; a local NVIDIA GPU is not required.

## Local benchmark simulation

Choose the benchmark and model explicitly:

```bash
dreamscale sim libero --model gr00t-n17-3b-libero --task-suite libero_object --task-id 0
dreamscale sim robocasa365 --model xiaomi-robotics-1-robocasa365 --task-suite atomic --task-id CloseFridge --task-seed 7 --max-steps 1800
dreamscale sim robocasa365 --model rldx-1-ft-rc365 --task-suite atomic --task-id CloseFridge
dreamscale sim robocasa365 --model dreamzero-droid --task-suite atomic --task-id CloseElectricKettleLid --max-duration 90
```

Simulation and robot CLI runs share `--keep-warm`: the default reserves loaded
compute for up to 300 seconds after the task, billed at the model's compute rate.
Use `--keep-warm 0` to release the reservation immediately, or specify 0–3600
seconds. A compatible subsequent run can reclaim your reserved worker without
loading the checkpoint again. The final sim output reports whether the server
parked the session, released it, or could not confirm the release. An open Python
SDK sim connection keeps its default of immediate release unless configured.

RoboCasa365 accepts `atomic` (65 tasks) or `composite` (300 tasks), with a task
name or a zero-based index within the selected suite. Xiaomi uses the reference
`pretrain` kitchen split, four observations spaced two physics steps apart, and
16-action chunks at 20 Hz. Inference runs asynchronously; while a request is
pending, the simulator stops base and arm motion and retains the gripper command.
DreamZero-DROID drives the same Franka arm with absolute joint targets at 15 Hz
from DROID's home pose. It sees the two agent views as its exterior cameras and the
hand camera as its wrist camera, and physics pauses while each 24-action chunk is
inferred. RoboCasa's robot-mounted views differ from DROID's training cameras, so
counter-height tasks suit it best; give it a longer `--max-duration`.

The first RoboCasa run uses `uv` to prepare a separate Python 3.12 native runtime
and download about 23 GiB of kitchen/object assets into `~/.dreamscale/sim/`.
Later runs reuse that cache. LIBERO uses the `sim` extra; RoboCasa prepares its
native dependencies independently. Add `--non-interactive` to run without a
viewer, or `--output-root DIRECTORY` to save the episode and timing evidence.
Installing the SDK does not grant model access; a funded beta account is required.

### MolmoSpaces (DROID)

MolmoSpaces runs local MuJoCo physics with cloud Cosmos3 DROID inference. It
prepares its own Python 3.11 runtime with `uv`; the base SDK is sufficient. Apple
Silicon macOS and x86_64 Linux support both classic MuJoCo and Filament. The first
run downloads the pinned simulator and its scene/object assets; later runs reuse
the cache. No local NVIDIA GPU is needed. Cosmos3 requires beta model access.

```bash
# One episode from each of the nine All Combined benchmarks (default episode 0).
dreamscale sim molmospaces --model cosmos3-nano-policy-droid

# A single pick episode, with video, timing and oracle-success artifacts.
dreamscale sim molmospaces --model cosmos3-nano-policy-droid --task-suite pick-v1.5 --task-id 0 --keep-warm 0 --output-root ./molmo-rollout

# Full All Combined evaluation: 8,915 episodes.
dreamscale sim molmospaces --model cosmos3-nano-policy-droid --task-suite all --task-id all --non-interactive --keep-warm 0 --output-root ./molmo-all

# Cosmos3 Edge, NVIDIA's 4B Cosmos3 DROID policy (private beta), on the same wire.
dreamscale sim molmospaces --model cosmos3-edge-policy-droid --task-suite pick-v1.5 --task-id 0 --keep-warm 0

# DreamZero-DROID (private beta): physics pauses while each chunk is inferred.
dreamscale sim molmospaces --model dreamzero-droid --task-suite close-v1 --task-id 0 --keep-warm 0
```

Suites: `open-v1`, `close-v1`, `pick-v1.5`, `pick-v2-classic`,
`pick-v2-filament`, `pick-v2-rand-cam`, `pnp-v2`, `pnp-next-to-v2`,
`pnp-color-v2`. Select an episode index or `all` with `--task-id`.
`--task-suite all` selects all nine; a numeric episode index runs that episode
in each suite. Close-v1 has 915 episodes and every other suite has 1,000.

The Cosmos3 models are `cosmos3-nano-policy-droid` and `cosmos3-edge-policy-droid`.
The adapter sends the current
exterior/wrist RGB views and seven measured Franka joints plus gripper state.
It duplicates the exterior view into DROID's second exterior slot, following
the upstream DreamZero evaluation convention. It executes complete 32-action
chunks at 15 Hz, holding measured joint positions while inference is pending.
MuJoCo uses the upstream-compatible 66 ms physics interval. Benchmark JSON owns
the episode initial state and horizon; success comes from upstream
`judge_success()`. `--max-steps` or `--max-duration` overrides the horizon for a
bounded run. A sample or overridden horizon is not a full benchmark score.

With `--output-root`, each episode gets a video and metrics bundle, and the
root `summary.json` records per-suite oracle success rates and their mean.
`full_benchmark` is true only when every selected episode runs with its original
horizon and seed. Inference reuses a session across episodes and renews compute
between episodes for long batches, before the provider lifetime limit.
`--keep-warm 0` closes the final session after the batch, including failures.

The implementation pins [MolmoSpaces v0.2.9](https://github.com/allenai/molmospaces/tree/v0.2.9).
See the [benchmark documentation](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1aRJ_NGWBzdLk3jJ71GvYx-dj1nbATQbDGfSG3V4Iy0g/edit)
for the official All Combined definition.

### RoboEval (bimanual Panda)

RoboEval runs its eight bimanual Franka Panda tasks in local MuJoCo with cloud
`rho-roboeval` inference. The first run uses `uv` to prepare a Python 3.12
runtime from [dreamscale-roboeval](https://github.com/Dreamscale-Labs/dreamscale-roboeval)
(about 1 GB, no asset downloads); later runs reuse it.

```bash
# Lift Pot, static variant.
dreamscale sim roboeval --model rho-roboeval

# Another task and variant, saving video and timing artifacts.
dreamscale sim roboeval --model rho-roboeval --task-suite pack_box --task-id PackBoxPosition --output-root ./roboeval-run
```

Tasks (`--task-suite`): `lift_pot`, `stack_two_blocks`, `cube_handover`,
`pick_single_book_from_table`, `stack_single_book_shelf`, `pack_box`,
`lift_tray`, `rotate_valve`. `--task-id` selects a variant by name or index;
0 is the static variant, then position, orientation and combined randomization.

The model receives the head and both wrist cameras at 224×224, both arms'
world-frame end-effector poses with 6D rotations and gripper openness, and the
task's training instruction. It returns 32 absolute poses with gripper close
commands at 20 Hz; the simulator executes the first 16, then replans, holding
the measured poses while inference is pending. Success is RoboEval's own task
check.

## SO-101 tasks

```bash
dreamscale robots so101 setup
dreamscale robots so101 calibrate
dreamscale run so101 --model flux-3-action-so101
```

`run so101` connects the arm, then asks for an instruction. Each task homes the arm to
the pose saved with `dreamscale robots so101 home --set`, previews one plan without
moving, runs on Enter until Ctrl+C, holds, and returns home. Without a saved home the arm
starts and ends each task where it is. When the command exits the arm keeps holding its
pose; support it, then run `dreamscale robots so101 release`. It supports
`flux-3-action-so101`, `molmoact2-so101`, `perceptron-isaac05-so101` and `pi0.5-so101`, for
organizations with access. The side camera is the policy's scene view: mount it fixed,
side-on, 60–80 cm from the arm with the lens just above prop height, so the whole arm
and workspace are in frame ([placement guide](https://docs.dreamscalelabs.com/robots/so101)).

`run so101` and `run yam` share one attended runner and the same options: `--mode
sync`, `--deployment`, `--region`, `--rtc`, `--context`, `--action-smoothing`,
`--trace-dir` and the rest. Joint limits are in each robot's units: `--max-joint-step`
(default 45) and `--max-joint-velocity` (default 150 per second) are degrees on SO-101.

After `dreamscale integrations hud setup`, `--hud` records each task as a HUD trace
with side and wrist camera video and the joints. You grade each task while the arm holds
its final pose, before it returns home; `--hud-job NAME` names the job.

## Bimanual YAM beta: setup and tasks

This beta requires organization access to the MolmoAct2 YAM model. Installing
the SDK does not grant model access; `dreamscale doctor yam` checks readiness.

For MolmoAct2, follow the [YAM hardware guide](dreamscale/guides/yam.md): a dedicated
Linux Python 3.12 install, guided LAN setup or `--cli` fallback, passive doctor,
and attended homing, prediction and execution. Enter confirms connection/homing
and execution; `cancel` exits. Settings and local controllers persist
between tasks. The [complete custom camera/controller example](dreamscale/examples/yam_custom.py)
also ships in the wheel and runs offline with `python -m dreamscale.examples.yam_custom`.
After `dreamscale integrations hud setup`, add `--hud` to `dreamscale run yam` to
[record and grade each task on HUD](dreamscale/guides/yam.md#record-and-grade-tasks-on-hud)
with camera video. Add `--inspect-robots` to
[record it as a graded Inspect Robots eval log](dreamscale/guides/yam.md#evaluate-with-inspect-robots).

## Managed LLM robot tasks

First follow the [YAM hardware install](dreamscale/guides/yam.md#install-on-the-robot-computer)
for a Linux Python 3.12 environment with camera dependencies and the pinned native
driver. An existing configured YAM installation can reuse its login and setup:

```bash
dreamscale login
dreamscale robots yam setup
dreamscale doctor yam --model gpt-6-astra
dreamscale run yam --model gpt-6-astra --instruction "Open both grippers"
```

The command uses the same attended YAM lifecycle as policy runs: confirm the
workspace, home, execute, then return home before disconnecting. Ctrl+C stops
the task and returns healthy arms home; Ctrl+C again interrupts that return.
Hardware faults, uncertain execution or an unconfirmed hold prevent automatic
return-home motion. Keep the run attended. Model access must be enabled for your account.
No separate provider API key is needed. Doctor checks hardware, account authentication
and local LLM compatibility without creating a session or generating tokens. The
run checks organization access and gateway readiness before the arming prompt.

The SO-101 runs the same managed LLMs through its own bounded tools (move the
gripper's tool point at most 8 cm per call, with its tilt and roll; open or close the
gripper), checked by the arm's kinematics and calibrated joint ranges:

```bash
dreamscale run so101 --model claude-fable-5-1 --instruction "put the marker in the cup"
```

Both robots take any managed LLM: `astra`, `sol`, `fable`, `opus` or `argon`
(Gemini 4 Argon, `--effort low|medium|high`), for example
`dreamscale run so101 --model argon --instruction "put the marker in the cup"`.

The same attended YAM flow is available in Python:

```python
import dreamscale

result = dreamscale.agents.run_yam(
    model="gpt-6-astra",
    instruction="Open both grippers",
)
```

Effort defaults to `medium` and speed to `standard`. Pass `effort=` or `speed=`
for supported model capabilities (`--effort` and `--speed` in the CLI).
There is **no default spending cap**. Set `max_cost=2.5` in Python or
`--max-cost 2.5` in the CLI to cap a run at 2.5 credits. Runs retain a limit of
100 provider calls or 30 minutes, and stop on completion, cancellation, a
provider failure, or uncertain physical execution. Progress shows elapsed time,
turns, public model summaries, requested motion and usage. In a terminal,
summaries wrap to the available width in muted grey, changed motion commands
appear in cyan, and a transient spinner marks the current provider request.
Unchanged gripper state is omitted. Public summaries may be absent; private
model reasoning is not exposed.

For another robot or a simulator, first qualify its tool profile with the managed
gateway, then implement `dreamscale.agents.RobotAdapter`
with `describe()`, async `observe()`, `execute(call)`, `stop()` and `close()`.
Import `RobotProfile`, `ToolSpec`, `Observation`, `ToolCall` and `ToolReceipt`
from `dreamscale.agents`. Enforce tool and motion bounds locally and report
measured results separately from commands sent. Then call:

```python
result = dreamscale.agents.run(
    adapter,
    model="gpt-6-astra",
    instruction="Pick up the cube",
)
# In an async application: result = await dreamscale.agents.arun(adapter, ...)
```

`run()` and `arun()` take ownership of stopping and closing the adapter on every
exit, including admission failure and cancellation. They reuse saved account
configuration and `DREAMSCALE_API_KEY`; explicit `api_key=` and
`control_plane_url=` overrides are available. Each run creates a private durable
journal under `~/.dreamscale/llm-runs/` (or `DREAMSCALE_HOME`). Existing journals
and motion identities are never replayed; start a fresh attended run after
uncertain execution. A terminal `TurnResult` reports model/provider status;
`model_reports_completion` is not independent proof of task success. An
uncertain `ToolReceipt` ends the run immediately. Reaching 100 calls returns
`None`; reaching 30 minutes raises `TimeoutError` after stopping the adapter.

Try the [packaged virtual counter adapter](dreamscale/examples/llm_counter.py)
offline, without hardware or an account:

```bash
python -m dreamscale.examples.llm_counter
```

The example also supports `--live --model gpt-6-astra --max-cost 2.5` once the
gateway has qualified its `virtual-counter/v1` profile. Production YAM uses
the qualified `yam-bimanual/v1` profile and SO-101 uses `so101/v1`.

LLM tools use the agent API; existing VLA action chunks continue to use
`dreamscale.connect()` and `predict()` below.

## Cloud policy inference

```python
import dreamscale

with dreamscale.connect(model="molmoact2-so101") as policy:
    result = policy.predict(
        observation,
        instruction="pick up the cube",
    )

print(len(result.actions), len(result.actions[0]))
```

`predict()` returns one action chunk and does not actuate a robot. Build
`observation` with the model-specific helper and validate every action locally
before adding a motion path.

`dreamscale.connect()` is the canonical SDK entrypoint.
There is no first-class public `connect_libero()` or `connect_franka()`
entrypoint; use `dreamscale.connect(model="molmoact2-libero")` or select another
checkpoint with `model=`.

Startup waits follow the server's fixed budget, including up to 60 minutes
when archived storage needs restoration. Pass a finite positive cap such as
`startup_timeout=1800` to `dreamscale.connect()` or `await dreamscale.aconnect()`
when your application needs a shorter wait. Older servers retain the legacy
900-second default.

Use a context manager so every cloud session closes deterministically:

```python
import dreamscale

with dreamscale.connect(model="molmoact2-libero") as policy:
    result = policy.run(
        instruction="put the mug on the plate",
        observe=observe,
        act=act,
        max_actions=220,
        strategy=dreamscale.RunStrategy.libero_default(),
    )
```

### Fresh current-position holds

Generic policies whose runtime contract uses current-position holds can read a
fresh target at the moment the action buffer underruns. Synchronous callers pass
a synchronous callback:

```python
def read_hold_action(context):
    return robot.read_joint_positions()

with dreamscale.connect(model="molmoact2-so101") as policy:
    result = policy.run(
        instruction="pick up the cube",
        observe=observe,
        act=act,
        read_hold_action=read_hold_action,
        max_actions=220,
    )
```

The async API also accepts an awaitable callback:

```python
async def read_hold_action(context):
    return await robot.read_joint_positions()

policy = await dreamscale.aconnect(model="molmoact2-so101")
async with policy:
    result = await policy.run(
        instruction="pick up the cube",
        observe=observe,
        act=act,
        read_hold_action=read_hold_action,
        max_actions=220,
    )
```

`read_hold_action` receives the stalled `RunContext` and runs only for an actual
underrun when `hold_behavior` is `current_position`. Selecting
`repeat_last_action` disables it. The result must contain exactly the runtime
contract's action dimension as finite real numeric scalars; invalid output raises
`ValueError` before hooks or actuation. DreamZero-DROID and DreamZero-YAM own
their managed hold paths and reject this callback explicitly.

### DreamZero-YAM quickstart

Install the `dreamzero` extra and sign in as shown above, then run the
[minimal DreamZero-YAM example](examples/dreamzero_yam.py):

```bash
python examples/dreamzero_yam.py
```

The example uses the canonical `dreamscale.connect("dreamzero-yam")` managed
loop and qualified defaults. Its callbacks are deliberately non-actuating:
replace `capture` with synchronized top, left, and right RGB frames plus the
current left-arm, left-gripper, right-arm, and right-gripper state. Camera
timestamps must describe the real captures using the same clock as
`time.time_ns()` and remain within 50 ms of one another.

After startup, the loop returns one 14-value absolute target at each native 30 Hz
control tick in `[left_j0..5, left_gripper, right_j0..5, right_gripper]` order;
it may initially hold the observed position while the first chunk is in flight.
Keep the context manager so the cloud session closes, and validate joint,
velocity, acceleration, workspace, collision, and gripper limits before
replacing the example's print callback with physical actuation.

### Deployments: your own fine-tunes

A deployment is your Isaac 0.5 fine-tune from Hugging Face, shaped to your robot and
deployed in a region. It is serverless: a GPU starts when a robot connects, and you pay per
session.

```bash
dreamscale deployments create hf://owner/repo --preset bimanual_yam   # or so101, or detected
dreamscale deployments create hf://owner/repo --preset so101 --region us-east
dreamscale deployments list
```

Without `--preset`, `create` takes the robot from the repo's deployment adapter;
`--camera key=view`, `--image-size HxW`, `--chunk` and `--hz` describe another robot. The
build checks the architecture, converts the weights and runs them once on a GPU (usually
about 15 minutes). `--region` is `us-west` (the default) or `us-east`; pick the one nearest
your robot. Then:

```python
with dreamscale.connect(
    "perceptron-isaac05", deployment="ckpt_…", region="available"
) as policy:
    ...
```

A deployment's name is `<account>/<slug>` (`acme-robotics/towel-fold`, shown by `dreamscale
deployments list`); run it by that name anywhere a model goes, `dreamscale run yam --model
acme-robotics/towel-fold` or `dreamscale.connect("acme-robotics/towel-fold")`, or by its bare
slug. Catalog models are `dreamscale/<model>` (the bare name works too).
`dreamscale deployments rename towel-fold folding-v2` renames one (the old name stops
working). A session runs a deployment only when it names it; without one, it runs the base
weights.

A π0.5 fine-tune (openpi or LeRobot) deploys the same way with `--base pi0.5`. Check what
the repo holds first; `create` fills every field from it and takes flags on top:

```bash
dreamscale deployments inspect hf://owner/repo --base pi0.5
dreamscale deployments create hf://owner/repo --base pi0.5 --preset arx5   # or bimanual_yam, so101, blank
dreamscale deployments rtc ckpt_… test-time   # or off, or trained --max-delay N
```

A preset fixes the robot's cameras and widths; `--preset blank --camera key=slot … --hz`
describes another robot. `--path checkpoints/55000` picks the checkpoint (default the
latest); `--horizon`, `--executed`, `--token-length`, `--no-state-in-prompt` and
`--delta-mask` its geometry; `--rtc off|test-time|trained` its RTC (test-time RTC adds
latency). The rate, chunk and widths come from the deployment, and a deployment serves one
robot at a time:

```python
with dreamscale.connect("pi0.5", deployment="ckpt_…") as policy:
    action = policy.next_action(dreamscale.robot.observation(cameras=frames, state=joints),
                                "fold the towel")
```

`dreamscale run yam --deployment ckpt_…` and `dreamscale run so101 --deployment ckpt_…` run
deployments built for those robots.

`endpoint=` and `checkpoint=` still work as older names for `deployment=`, and
`dreamscale endpoints …` and `dreamscale checkpoints …` for `dreamscale deployments …`.

### Running chunks on your own clock

`next_action()` buffers chunks, requests the next one early and blends them. A robot whose
own software runs whole chunks calls `predict()` instead and does that itself:

- Row 0 of a chunk is the first action to execute after `chunk.capture_time_ns` (this
  computer's `time.time_ns()`; pass `capture_time_ns=` to the observation helper when the
  frames are older than the call). Row `i` follows `i / policy.contract.action_hz` seconds
  later.
- With train-time RTC (`policy.contract.rtc == "train_time"`), pass the actions the robot
  will still execute after the new observation as `committed_actions`. The server pins as
  many as it expects to play before the chunk arrives, at most
  `policy.contract.max_prefix_rows`, and reports how many in `chunk.prefix_rows`; they come
  back exactly as sent. Without them, each chunk starts fresh.
- Models that read the ticks before the observation (FLUX 3 Action among them) take them as
  `past_observations` (the observations of the control ticks just before, oldest first) and
  `action_history` (the actions executed on them): `policy.contract.past_observations` and
  `.action_history` say how many. At an episode's start pass `[]`; the SDK pads as the model
  was trained and reports how many ticks it filled in as `chunk.history_padded`.

```python
c = policy.contract
chunk = policy.predict(
    observation, "drive to the cone",
    past_observations=list(ticks)[len(ticks) - c.past_observations:],
    action_history=list(done)[len(done) - c.action_history:],
    committed_actions=current.actions[row:] if c.rtc == "train_time" else None,
)
row = int((time.time_ns() - chunk.capture_time_ns) * c.action_hz // 1e9)
```

### Startup and archived checkpoints

`connect()`, `aconnect()`, prewarm and CLI callers use the server's startup budget by default. An archived checkpoint can take up to 60 minutes to restore; progress reports the storage phase and elapsed time. Restoration itself does not charge customer credits. The compute allocation/readiness timers start after storage is restored, within the fixed overall deadline.

Set `startup_timeout` explicitly on `connect` or `aconnect` when your caller needs a shorter wait. It caps the server budget and cannot extend it. Older servers without a startup budget retain the SDK's legacy default. Administrator hotload holds a specific execution profile for the next compatible customer; your own `keep_warm` reservation still has priority.

### Keep compute warm between connections

Pass `keep_warm` as a number of seconds to reserve the same loaded compute after
the connection closes:

```python
with dreamscale.connect(
    model="dreamzero-yam",
    idle_timeout=3600,
    keep_warm=900,
) as policy:
    result = policy.predict(observation, instruction="fold the towel")
```

`keep_warm` defaults to `300` seconds, the same as the CLI's `--keep-warm`, and accepts integer values from `0` to
`3600`; pass `0` to disable it. It is a duration, not a boolean. The reserved
period remains billable
because the GPU is held exclusively for that session. A later compatible
`connect()` by the same user reclaims the exact AWS worker or exact Modal
function call with a rotated session token, avoiding model or engine reload.
The request must still match the model, resolved optimization profile, and
region-routing constraints; otherwise normal placement is used.

`idle_timeout` is separate: it limits time without inference while a client is
connected. If it ends a connection configured with `keep_warm`, the resource
parks for the requested warm duration. Closing a connection drains accepted
inference before parking, while queued work is discarded. An explicit
`dreamscale sessions stop`/session DELETE, access revocation, exhausted credit, or
provider failure always ends the reservation. A legacy worker that does not
support parking safely closes instead. Provider hard lifetimes can shorten a
reservation near their own deadline; Dreamscale never promises a keep-warm window
beyond its conservative 24-hour Modal execution deadline. Independently, an
attached Modal runtime exits after 3900 seconds without control-plane activity;
it first drains any accepted inference, and current control traffic renews that
local safety deadline.

### DreamZero sampling

DreamZero uses one existing `RunStrategy` field; the server owns temporal
admission and the SDK continues uploading observations while asynchronous
inference is outstanding.

| Mode | Behavior | YAM | DROID |
|---|---|---|---|
| `upstream_eval` | Boundary capture, exact response wait, then eight actions | Explicit | Explicit |
| `async_8` | Single-flight async admission after a newer eight-tick epoch | Compatibility and rollback | Default |
| `async_latest` | Single-flight, latest-only admission on the newest strictly newer tick | Accepted default | Rejected |

An omitted value means “use the qualified default for this model”; it does not
disable sampling. YAM resolves omission to `async_latest`; DROID remains
`async_8`. Explicit compatibility and reference selections remain available:

```python
# YAM accepted default
strategy = dreamscale.RunStrategy()

# Explicit compatibility mode
strategy = dreamscale.RunStrategy(dreamzero_sampling="async_8")

# Synchronous reference/evaluation
strategy = dreamscale.RunStrategy(dreamzero_sampling="upstream_eval")
```

In a managed `upstream_eval` run, the SDK waits for the eight-action boundary,
captures and uploads one observation, waits for the chunk sourced from that
exact observation, and then executes the next eight aligned actions at 15 Hz.
The inference/network delay is an intentional pause in this reference mode;
there is no continuous upload or action/inference overlap. Externally clocked
`step()` callers may still provide every environment observation, but only
ticks `0, 8, 16, ...` are uploaded. The server—not the client—continues to
construct T1 and exact eight-tick-history T4 windows.

`async_latest` does not create a client request queue. The SDK retains one
latest unsent observation, while the YAM server admits at most one inference
and collapses observations received while busy to the newest eligible control
tick. Returned `PolicyStepResult` values expose `source_control_tick` and
`source_capture_to_execution_ms`; hold actions without a source chunk report
`None` for both.

The server returns absolute `24x14` YAM targets. For `async_latest`, the SDK
keeps the existing two committed steps and motion-smooths only the remaining
temporally aligned suffix: it blends adjacent absolute-target motion using 85%
new and 15% old motion, then corrects toward the new absolute target over five
steps. It never shifts or rebases a chunk. YAM grippers remain continuous
absolute targets and are handled with the other 12 joints. `async_8`,
`upstream_eval`, and DROID retain direct commit-prefix replacement. At each
upstream boundary, the previous chunk's unexecuted suffix is discarded before
the exact new chunk is installed, so upstream evaluation adds no smoothing,
averaging, shifting, or rebasing confound.

DreamZero keeps its smoothing choice internal to this qualified mode; explicit
DreamZero smoothing weights or modes remain unsupported. A managed `run()`
callback may return `ActResult(accepted_action=...)` so the next merge anchors
to the target accepted by the actuator. Externally clocked `step()` callers
without actuator feedback anchor to the requested target.

### Action smoothing

`policy.run()` smooths overlapping chunks automatically from the model's action
space without changing the selected control mode or its inference cadence:

```python
strategy = dreamscale.RunStrategy()  # action_smoothing="auto"
```

`auto` resolves to `output` for delta-action models such as LIBERO and to
`motion` for absolute-joint models such as SO101. The raw-absolute YAM contract
is also an absolute target space, although DreamZero owns the narrower mode
selection described above. `output` averages old and new
action values at each overlapping logical timestep, using 70% new by default.
`motion` blends consecutive target increments, using 85% new motion by default,
then corrects gradually toward the new absolute targets. Absolute-position
models still receive absolute-position targets; `motion` changes only how
overlapping client trajectories are combined.

For controlled comparisons, set `action_smoothing="output"` or
`action_smoothing="motion"`. `motion` requires an
`absolute_joint_position` or `raw_absolute_joint` contract. Its default correction horizon is
`max(3, ceil(chunk_size / 5))` action steps, so a 30-action SO101 chunk resolves
to six steps. This is a recurring 1/6 correction gain, not a finite 200 ms
transition. Optional expert overrides are `smoothing_new_weight` and
`smoothing_correction_steps`. Existing `motion` tuning does not adapt to latency.

MolmoAct2 YAM also offers two opt-in experimental modes: `motion_handover`
ends a disagreement-dependent correction exactly, and `motion_speed` reduces
damping for slower intra-chunk arm motion. Their grippers follow incoming targets
independently of arm-speed adaptation. See the [YAM async guide](dreamscale/guides/yam-async.md)
for equations, limits, CLI commands and saved Rerun recordings. `auto` is unchanged.

Returned actions remain aligned to their original logical timesteps. Expired
prefix steps are skipped, and a chunk that is entirely stale is discarded; it
is never shifted forward and replayed. With eager inference, a predicted action
can therefore be superseded or expire without ever being dispatched. This is
intentional: inference stays reactive while the action loop executes only the
currently resolved timestep.

For physical arms that clamp a requested target, return the accepted target so
the next smoothing pass starts from what the robot was actually asked to
execute:

```python
def act(action, context):
    accepted = robot.send_and_return_accepted_target(action)
    return dreamscale.ActResult(accepted_action=accepted)
```

`accepted_action` must be finite and have the same dimension as the requested
action. Existing callbacks returning `None`, `bool`, or `ActResult(done=...)`
remain supported.

`policy.run()` returns one `RunResult` and prints the same run-scoped summary
immediately. Its counters and stop condition are deliberately narrow:

- `result.done` means the `act()` callback requested a stop; it does not assert
  task success or completion.
- `result.actions` counts control callback steps, not confirmed physical robot
  execution.
- On Ctrl+C or failure, completed callbacks and attempted callbacks remain
  distinct. The partial result reaches `RunHooks.on_summary` and
  `policy.last_run_result`; the original exception is preserved. Startup holds
  and execution underruns are counted separately.
- `result.policy_calls` counts completed policy responses accepted by that run.
- `result.timing.policy_response_ms` measures SDK observation submission through
  action-chunk receipt. It excludes observation construction and physical
  actuation.
- `result.timing.data_plane_rtt_ms` is an authenticated application-path round
  trip measured with a clock probe. It includes transport framing and any
  load-balancer or relay hops; it is not a one-way network-latency estimate.
- Worker queue, preprocessing, inference, and postprocessing summaries are
  derived from per-response worker timestamps. Missing samples remain missing
  rather than being inferred from client-side residual time.

`region="auto"` is the default (`"nearest"` is its older name) and pins the lowest
measured HTTPS-latency region, even when another region is already warm.
`region="available"` reuses compatible warm compute in any measured candidate
region before starting cold compute. Any other value is a region id such as
`"us-west"` (`GET /v1/regions` lists them; the AWS-style `"us-west-2"` still
works) and is an exact constraint with no regional fallback.
The latency check uses five serial samples per region and reports when partial
or failed evidence required a deterministic fallback.

`transport="auto"` uses an assigned session tunnel, or tries QUIC with hosted relay fallback;
`transport="quic"` requires QUIC, and `transport="relay"` uses the relay
directly.

### Advanced SO101 placement

The service's SO101 `region="available"` launch policy tries AWS Sydney, then
Modal Australia, then Modal us-west. Existing clients already support this route
and its assigned WebSocket tunnel with `transport="auto"`.

Starting with 0.1.0a96, `connect()` and `aconnect()` accept an
optional hard `provider="aws"` or `provider="modal"` constraint for
`molmoact2-so101`. Omit it for normal service-managed placement. For example:

```python
with dreamscale.connect(
    "molmoact2-so101",
    provider="modal",
    region="us-west-2",
    transport="auto",
    keep_warm=900,
) as policy:
    result = policy.predict(observation, instruction="Pick up the pen")
```

| Provider | Region | Constraint |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `aws` | `ap-southeast-2` / `us-west-2` | AWS Sydney / us-west only |
| `aws` | `available` / `nearest` | Existing AWS availability / latency selection |
| `modal` | `ap-southeast-2` | Modal Australia only |
| `modal` | `us-west-2` | Modal us-west only |
| `modal` | `available` | Modal Australia, then us-west |

These are routing groups for Modal, not promises of physical AWS placement.
Modal uses qualified H100 artifacts and requires `transport="auto"`.
`provider="modal", region="nearest"` is unsupported because AWS HTTPS probes
do not measure Modal's locations. Exhaustion never relaxes an explicit provider
or region constraint. Warm reuse respects the same constraints.

For `molmoact2-bimanual-yam`, the qualified us-west launch policy uses AWS
us-west L4, followed by Modal us-west L4 when AWS cannot start. Select
`region="available", transport="auto", rtc="off"` to use this route. These
YAM provider controls require SDK 0.1.0a96 or later:

```python
with dreamscale.connect(
    "molmoact2-bimanual-yam",
    region="available",
    transport="auto",
    rtc="off",
    keep_warm=60,
    startup_timeout=450,
) as policy:
    chunk = policy.predict(observation, instruction="Fold the cloth")
```

For diagnosis, add `provider="aws"` or `provider="modal"`; `region="us-west-2"`
then limits selection to AWS us-west or the Modal us-west routing group.
YAM has no Australian or H100 fallback. Modal requires `transport="auto"` and
uses an authenticated WSS tunnel. Explicit `keep_warm=0` releases the reservation;
300 seconds is the default and 3,600 seconds the maximum. Fallback happens during
startup; a running session is never migrated to another provider.

The advanced selector checks server support before creating compute. An older
control plane produces an explicit error; the SDK never drops the selector.
SDK 0.1.0a96 supports automatic fallback but does not
have this optional argument.

### π0.5 endpoints and `speed`

`pi0.5-bimanualyam` and `pi0.5-so101` are separate π0.5 endpoints with their own contracts.

`pi0.5-bimanualyam` serves the π0.5 train-time-RTC checkpoint (openpi JAX) on Modal
us-west H100: 30-action chunks at 30 Hz. `rtc="auto"` uses its train-time
late-bound prefix; `rtc="off"` turns it off. `pi0.5-so101` is the same runtime on
one SO-101 arm (`dreamscale run so101 --model pi0.5-so101`), with RTC off at
launch. Both run only on Modal: they reject `provider="aws"` and regions other
than `nearest`, `available` and `us-west-2`. Use a current SDK
(`pip install -U dreamscale`): older releases don't know the `pi0.5-*` names.

`speed` selects a hardware tier per connection: `pi0.5-bimanualyam` accepts `speed="fast"`,
served from the same H100 pool; `pi0.5-so101` has no fast tier.

```python
with dreamscale.connect("pi0.5-bimanualyam", speed="fast") as policy:
    chunk = policy.predict(observation, instruction="Fold the towel")
```

`speed="fast"` never falls back to L4. If no H100 can start, `connect()` fails
instead of running slower hardware at the fast price. It rejects `provider="aws"`
and regions other than `available` and `us-west-2`, and `region="nearest"`
becomes `available`. Fast requires `transport="auto"`. Before creating compute,
the SDK confirms the server supports fast. A session the server does not report
as fast is deleted and raises an error, so a fast request is never silently
downgraded. `speed="fast"` requires SDK 0.1.0a96 or later. Both tiers serve the
same model; fast shortens each inference, not the action contract.

## MolmoAct2-DROID on Franka

MolmoAct2-DROID consumes an exterior RGB view and wrist RGB view. A second
exterior view is optional; when omitted, Dreamscale reuses the first exterior
frame for the checkpoint's second exterior slot.

Robot state is seven Franka joint positions in radians followed by a gripper
value in `[0, 1]`.

```python
import numpy as np
import dreamscale

exterior_rgb = np.zeros((480, 640, 3), dtype=np.uint8)
wrist_rgb = np.zeros((480, 640, 3), dtype=np.uint8)

observation = dreamscale.franka.observation(
    exterior_frame=exterior_rgb,
    wrist_frame=wrist_rgb,
    joint_positions=[0.0] * 7,
    gripper=0.5,  # 0=open, 1=closed
)

with dreamscale.connect(model="molmoact2-droid") as policy:
    result = policy.predict(
        observation,
        instruction="pick up the green block",
    )

assert len(result.actions) == 15
assert all(len(action) == 8 for action in result.actions)
```

The checkpoint returns a 15-step chunk at 15 Hz. Each action is an absolute
target `[joint_0, ..., joint_6, gripper]`; joints are radians and the gripper is
in `[0, 1]`.

`predict()` does not actuate hardware or provide a Franka safety controller.
Validate joint, velocity, acceleration, workspace, collision, and gripper
limits before sending any target to a robot.

## Manual action loop

For a caller-owned control loop, pass the task instruction to
`policy.next_action(...)`. Dreamscale owns inference, refill, action buffering,
calibration, and RTC prefix context; the caller owns sensing, actuation, and
loop cadence.

```python
import time

dt = 1.0 / policy.action_hz
while running:
    tick = time.perf_counter()
    action = policy.next_action(
        observe(),
        instruction="put the mug on the plate",
    )
    robot.execute(action)
    time.sleep(max(0.0, dt - (time.perf_counter() - tick)))
```

## CLI

Sign in once. Credentials are stored in `~/.dreamscale/config.toml`; an existing
`~/.dropbear` from an earlier install is used in place.

```bash
dreamscale login
dreamscale models list
```

`dreamscale models list` lists the models your organization can run: type (VLA, WAM
or LLM), the robots and simulation benchmarks each runs on, its rate, and
whether compute is already warm. `--verbose` adds a row under each model for every
region, provider and variant that serves it, with its live state. `--json` prints
the same rows for scripts.

For headless setup, use bare `--api-key` to paste a key into a hidden prompt:

```bash
dreamscale login --api-key
```

Each machine holds one login key: logging in again revokes the key the last login saved.
Login keys don't appear on the API keys page. `dreamscale auth whoami` shows the account
and organization you are signed in as, and `dreamscale auth logout` revokes the machine's
login key and removes it:

```bash
dreamscale auth whoami
dreamscale auth logout
```

Run robot-neutral setup checks:

```bash
dreamscale doctor
```

SO-101 and simulation checks are explicit:

```bash
dreamscale doctor so101
dreamscale doctor sim
```

Install shell completion with:

```bash
dreamscale --install-completion
```

Useful session commands:

```bash
dreamscale sessions list
dreamscale sessions stop <session-id>
dreamscale sessions stop --all
```

Documentation: https://docs.dreamscalelabs.com

Rho-LIBERO uses the same local simulator path:

```bash
dreamscale sim libero --model rho-libero --task-suite libero_spatial --task-id 0
```

It uses the checkpoint's 16-step horizon at 20 Hz. Raw simulator RGB images are
rotated 180 degrees once at the SDK boundary and encoded at 256×256. For a custom
LIBERO environment, pass `model="rho-libero"` to `dreamscale.libero.observation()`.
Rho inference dependencies run on the worker and are not SDK dependencies.

GR00T N1.7 LIBERO uses the `libero_object` suite. With beta access, a funded
organization and a saved login or `DREAMSCALE_API_KEY`, run:

```bash
uvx --python 3.12 --from 'dreamscale[sim]==0.1.0a96' dreamscale sim libero --model gr00t-n17-3b-libero --task-suite libero_object --task-id 0 --max-steps 300 --non-interactive
```

LIBERO physics runs locally at 20 Hz while the cloud policy predicts action
chunks asynchronously. Transport is selected automatically. RTC is off for this
checkpoint. Task IDs 0–9 are available; use `--output-root <directory>` to retain
rollout artifacts.

LingBot VA LIBERO Long uses the `libero_10` suite. With beta access, a funded
organization and a saved login or `DREAMSCALE_API_KEY`, run:

```bash
uvx --python 3.12 --from 'dreamscale[sim]==0.1.0a96' dreamscale sim libero --model lingbot-va-libero --task-suite libero_10 --task-id 0 --max-steps 2400 --non-interactive
```

The simulator runs locally at 20 Hz and requests action chunks asynchronously
from a cloud B200 worker. The 2,400-tick budget allows 120 seconds, including
inference waits. It holds position while waiting for a new chunk;
20 Hz simulation does not mean a new inference result every 50 ms. Cold startup
includes checkpoint loading and compilation. The worker shuts down after idle
retention, and each session starts with fresh model state. Task IDs 0–9 are
available; use `--output-root <directory>` to retain the rollout artifacts.

RLWRLD's RLDX-1 fine-tunes run on both simulators. With access, a funded
organization and a saved login or `DREAMSCALE_API_KEY`, run:

```bash
uvx --python 3.12 --from 'dreamscale[sim]==0.1.0a96' dreamscale sim libero --model rldx-1-ft-libero --task-suite libero_spatial --task-id 0 --non-interactive
uvx --python 3.12 dreamscale@0.1.0a96 sim robocasa365 --model rldx-1-ft-rc365 --task-suite atomic --task-id CloseFridge --non-interactive
```

Each policy call sends four frames per camera (simulator steps -6, -4, -2 and 0)
and returns sixteen actions; the first eight execute before the next call, as in
RLWRLD's benchmark harness. The simulator holds while a call is in flight.

`dreamscale run yam --model rho-yam-box` reuses the existing YAM rig config
and the built-in end-effector/gripper profile for standard YAM arms with
LINEAR_4310 grippers. Changed tools or geometry require measured overrides. See the [YAM guide](dreamscale/guides/yam.md#rho-yam-end-effector-control)
for the calibrated I2RT adapter contract.
The checkpoint generates 50 actions; managed and manual SDK queues execute
only its declared first 25. `predict()` returns the raw generated chunk for
inspection. User-managed execution must honor `runtime_contract.execution_chunk_size`.
